(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-03"] = {"6.2":{"s":"A circle is the set of points at a fixed distance, the radius, from a fixed point, the centre. With the centre at the origin the equation is x² + y² = r². With the centre at (h, k) it is (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r². Expanding gives the general form with 2gx and 2fy, whose centre is (−g, −f). The circle is real only if g² + f² is more than c.","f":[{"x":"x^2 + y^2 = r^2","n":"Standard circle with centre (0, 0) and radius r. In parametric form x = r*cos(theta), y = r*sin(theta).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"},{"x":"(x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2","n":"Centre-radius form: centre (h, k), radius r.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"},{"x":"x^2 + y^2 + 2*g*x + 2*f*y + c = 0","n":"General form: centre (-g, -f), radius sqrt(g^2 + f^2 - c), real if g^2 + f^2 > c.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"}],"w":["In the general form the centre is (−g, −f): the signs flip, and the coefficients are 2g and 2f, not g and f.","Radius = √(g² + f² − c). If this is negative, there is no real circle."]},"6.3":{"s":"A tangent to a circle touches it at exactly one point, and it is perpendicular to the radius at that point. For the circle x² + y² = r², the tangent at a point (x1, y1) on it is found by replacing x² by x·x1 and y² by y·y1. In parametric form, the tangent at the point with parameter θ1 is also a short linear equation. The slope form of a tangent has the slope m given.","f":[{"x":"x*x_1 + y*y_1 = r^2","n":"Tangent to x^2 + y^2 = r^2 at the point (x_1, y_1) on the circle.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"},{"x":"x*cos(theta_1) + y*sin(theta_1) = r","n":"Tangent at the point with parameter theta_1, using x = r*cos(theta), y = r*sin(theta).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"},{"x":"y = m*x ± sqrt(r^2*m^2 + r^2)","n":"Tangent of slope m to x^2 + y^2 = r^2: two parallel tangents.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"}],"w":["The point must lie on the circle; for a point outside, two tangents can be drawn.","The slope form has two signs: there are two tangents with the same slope, one on each side."]},"6.4":{"s":"The line y = mx + c touches the circle x² + y² = r² only when c has a particular size. Put the line into the circle, or use the fact that the distance from the centre to a tangent equals the radius, and the condition follows. The same condition gives the slope form of the tangent, and the point where the line touches the circle can be found from it.","f":[{"x":"c^2 = r^2*m^2 + r^2","n":"Condition for y = m*x + c to be tangent to x^2 + y^2 = r^2. The point of contact is (-r^2*m/c, r^2/c).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"}],"w":["The condition has r² on both terms: c² = r²(m² + 1). A common slip is to leave out the second r²."]},"6.5":{"s":"The director circle of a conic is the path of the points from which two perpendicular tangents can be drawn to it. For a circle with centre at the origin and radius r, the director circle has the same centre and a larger radius, √2 times r. So its equation has 2r² on the right-hand side. The two tangents meet at right angles at every point of this circle.","f":[{"x":"x^2 + y^2 = 2*r^2","n":"Director circle of the circle x^2 + y^2 = r^2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"}],"w":["The director circle has radius r√2, so the right side is 2r², not 2r."]}};
